WEB SOURCE (the specification)
  web/src/components/ui/DataTableResizer.tsx — a PURE presentational column-resize drag handle that
  follows the WAI-ARIA "Window Splitter Pattern" (role="separator" + aria-valuenow/min/max + tabIndex=0):
    props   : columnKey, width, minWidth=60, maxWidth=800, onResize(next), onResizeEnd?(final), label?
    clamp   : max(minWidth, min(maxWidth, round(n)))
    drag    : pointer down captures startX/startWidth → move fires onResize(clamp(startWidth+delta))
              → up/cancel fires onResizeEnd(width)
    keyboard: ArrowLeft/Right = ±8 px, Home = 80 px, End = maxWidth — each fires onResize + onResizeEnd
    a11y    : aria-label = label ?? "Resize column {columnKey}"; dragging adds opacity-100 bg-cyan-400/60
    note    : onClick stops propagation so the handle never triggers the header sort.
  No data hooks. No loading / empty / error / stale / offline branches — those belong to the table that
  embeds the handle, not to the handle. The prompt itself records "Data sources: (none — pure
  presentational component)" and "Shared components used: (none)" and "Charts / maps used: (none)".
  The genuine states the web source carries (idle vs. dragging, + keyboard focus, + a non-resizable edge)
  are ALL reproduced — see PARITY MAPPING. The Windows sibling (apps/windows/.../TsDataTableResizer.cs)
  resolves the same prompt the same way (a focused handle, KeyboardStep 8, min-clamp, accessible name).

